===Alternative forms===
* [[Bohdan]]

===Etymology===
{{unreferenced}}
It was formed from:
* {{term|Бог||God|tr=Bog|sc=Cyrl}}, of Scythian/Indo-Iranian origin; its original meaning was riches, abundance, and good fortune, as it was still used in the name of [[Baghdad]] and in the Slavic {{term|богатъ|sc=Cyrs|tr=bogatŭ||rich}}; however with time it became a title of Mithra and then from there it replaced the Slavic Indo-European<!-- what language—PS or PIE? --> name of the celestial God *Deivos (Latin {{term|Deus|lang=la}}, Sanskrit {{term||tr=Deva|lang=sk}}{{rfscript|Deva}}, German {{term|Ziu|lang=de}}) some time after the split between the Baltic and Slavic languages (as Lithuanian still keeps {{term|Dievas|lang=lt}}).
* {{term|дан||gift||tr=dan|sc=Cyrl}}, of Indo-European origins, akin to Latin {{term|donum|lang=la}}, Greek {{term|δῶρον||tr=dōron|lang=grc}}, Welsh {{term|dawn|lang=cy}} or Irish {{term|dán|lang=ga}}.

There are also some other languages in which we can find names meaning "gift of God", such as Greek ([[Theodore]]), Latin ([[Adeodatus]] and [[Deusdedit]]), Arabic ({{Arab|[[عطاء الله]]}}, [[Ataullah]]) and Hebrew ([[Jonathan]] and [[Nathaniel]]).

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{given name|male|from=Slavonic}} of {{etyl|sla}} origin meaning "gift of God".

